WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.220
Death Stranding is an upcoming game from Metal
Gear Solid mastermind Hideo Kojima — his

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.750
first since his dramatic breakup with Konami
a couple years back.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.570
If you've seen the trailers, you know it stars
The Walking Dead's Norman Reedus, Rogue One's

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.969
Mads Mikkelsen, and visionary director Guillermo
del Toro.

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:19.640
We know from interviews that the game takes
place in an open-world setting, and will have

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.670
some kind of multiplayer component, in addition
to a lengthy single-player campaign.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:28.010
And yet, when it comes to specifics, Kojima
plays things close to the vest.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:32.029
Death Stranding looks like it's going to be
an unusual experience, and Kojima is doing

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:36.350
everything he can to keep the mystery alive
until the game's as-yet unknown release date.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.210
Still, occasionally, a few details slip through.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:40.210
For example ...

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:41.600
Whale tale?

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.609
Death Stranding is all about connections … and
maybe a little bit about whales.

00:00:45.609 --> 00:00:49.449
In a typically mysterious interview with Wired,
Kojima says that "The story is about what

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:52.769
you call 'strands' in psychology — how people
are connected."

00:00:52.769 --> 00:00:57.940
Kojima says Death Stranding involves connecting
players to one another via gameplay, and explores

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:02.620
Kojima's ideas about "trying to connect one
character to another, or to connect life and

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.620
death."

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.920
The title also might have something to do
with whales.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.219
The first trailer opens on a beach full of
dead wildlife, including dead whales.

00:01:10.219 --> 00:01:13.860
When whales beach themselves, marine biologists
call it "stranding."

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.890
Experts think the giant creatures may do it
to escape predators, or they may just be lost

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.409
— but it rarely ends well.

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:23.219
The whales typically die of dehydration or
die when the high tide comes in.

00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:28.480
What all this has to do with the story of
Death Stranding, however, remains a mystery.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.480
Anime inspiration?

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:33.229
Clearly, Kojima has 1988 anime classic Akira
on the brain.

00:01:33.229 --> 00:01:37.250
When fans asked when Death Stranding is going
to come out, Kojima Productions replied on

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.760
Twitter: "Before the Tokyo Olympic Games.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:40.829
Before the new Akira."

00:01:40.829 --> 00:01:44.950
The Tokyo Olympics are in 2020, but the Akira
reference isn't as clear.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.870
Warner Bros. has been trying to get a live-action
Akira film off the ground for years, but the

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.969
movie isn't officially in production, and
has no definite release date.

00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:55.210
So it could just be a joke...but it might
also be a clue.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:56.450
"Yeah, yeah.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:57.509
Don't make me laugh."

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:02.000
It's a bit of a stretch, but some fans think
they've found Akira connections in Death Stranding's

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.359
two trailers, as well.

00:02:03.359 --> 00:02:07.170
The chunky tank from the second trailer looks
vaguely like the tanks in Akira.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:11.480
And like Akira, Death Stranding looks like
it's set in a world ravaged by nuclear war,

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.050
and clearly has something to do with human
experimentation.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:16.050
Gamechanger

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:20.540
We know, at a very basic level, that Death
Stranding is going to be an "action" title,

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.269
and that it won't be a horror game like Kojima's
cancelled collaboration with Reedus and del

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:25.860
Toro, Silent Hills.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.830
Beyond that, what players actually do in the
game is still unknown — but Kojima hints

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:33.019
that he's trying to create a new genre, or
at least subgenre.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:37.970
Talking to Digital Spy in June 2016, Kojima
says people only called the original Metal

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:42.379
Gear a "stealth" game years later, once the
industry realized it needed a name to describe

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:44.180
the games inspired by it.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.709
Kojima says Death Stranding is a similar case,
telling Digital Spy, "The player will be controlling

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:51.940
Norman, but by playing it, they will find
something different, something that won't

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.580
fit in established terms."

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:58.069
Referencing a Japanese story, Kojima uses
a rope and a stick as metaphor, saying early

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:02.400
humans used the stick to "keep away bad things,"
while the rope's purpose was "to keep good

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.489
things close."

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:07.900
Currently, most video games focus on the "stick"
— punches, kicks, guns, and other forms

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:08.900
of aggression.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.040
With Death Stranding, Kojima wants to make
a game with a lot more rope, metaphorically

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.040
speaking.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.909
But it's still unclear how that idea will
translate to actual gameplay.

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:18.909
Baby boom

00:03:18.909 --> 00:03:23.370
For some reason, Death Stranding's promotional
materials are chock-full of babies and baby

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:24.370
dolls.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.530
In the first trailer, Norman Reedus picks
up a lost baby on a beach, cradling it to

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:29.390
his body before it disappears.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:33.690
In the second, Guillermo del Toro cradles
a womb-like capsule with a living baby inside

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.110
while a mangled doll floats at his feet.

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:40.220
Kojima Productions filmed both trailers using
motion capture technology, and didn't use

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.270
real babies in the production of either video.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.640
On set, dolls stood in for the infants.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.079
In a series of tweets, Kojima revealed that
the prop baby held by Norman Reedus is named

00:03:49.079 --> 00:03:50.079
"Lu number two."

00:03:50.079 --> 00:03:52.430
The other baby doll used is named "Lu number
one 1."

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:56.329
It's unclear if the name "Lu" has any significance
whatsoever, but when the details are this

00:03:56.329 --> 00:03:58.450
scarce, we'll take what we can get.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:03:59.450
Engine twins

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:05.319
2017's open world sci-fi smash Horizon Zero
Dawn set a new, very high bar for video game

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:08.799
graphics, thanks largely to Guerrilla Games'
proprietary Decima Engine.

00:04:08.799 --> 00:04:13.540
So it's no surprise that Kojima wanted to
use Decima to bring Death Stranding to life.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:17.769
Instead of signing a contract or demanding
a fee, however, Guerrilla gave Kojima access

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:19.000
to the engine for free.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.890
So if you enjoyed the look and feel of Horizon
Zero Dawn, expect Death Stranding to have

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.250
a similar feel, but with a Kojima twist.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:29.621
There is one catch to this arrangement, but
it's more like a bonus: any changes that Kojima

00:04:29.621 --> 00:04:33.949
makes to the Decima engine's code has to be
shared with Guerrilla, allowing both companies

00:04:33.949 --> 00:04:35.190
to develop it together.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.860
According to Kojima, it's a deal that's worked
out beautifully.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:42.580
After six months, he says the code "completely
merged together" into something new and improved.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:47.449
At Playstation Experience 2016, Kojima, through
a translator, said Guerilla's ambition is

00:04:47.449 --> 00:04:48.449
extraordinary:

00:04:48.449 --> 00:04:51.110
"Making games, in a way, is like going to
space."

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.479
"Guerilla came and was like, 'Let's go to
Jupiter together.'

00:04:53.479 --> 00:04:55.240
So that was a great feeling."

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:56.240
Thanks for watching!

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.380
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.439
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

